The module compiles cleanly. The implementation uses the fact that for each bit *i*, `(q<<1)` supplies `q[i-1]` (with bit 0 = 0, matching boundary `q[-1]=0`) and `(q>>1)` supplies `q[i+1]` (with bit 511 = 0, matching boundary `q[512]=0`), so their XOR is exactly Rule 90.

```verilog
module TopModule (
    input          clk,
    input          load,
    input  [511:0] data,
    output [511:0] q
);

    reg [511:0] q_reg;

    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (load)
            q_reg <= data;
        else
            // Rule 90: next[i] = q[i-1] ^ q[i+1], boundaries are zero
            q_reg <= (q_reg << 1) ^ (q_reg >> 1);
    end

    assign q = q_reg;

endmodule
```
